% Precedence, associativity, and the operator families.

### name: op.prec.mul-over-add
--- source
2 + 3 * 4
--- expect
num 14
===
### name: op.prec.concat-looser-than-add
--- source
"a" & 1 + 2
--- expect
text "a3"
===
### name: op.prec.concat-tighter-than-compare
--- source
"a" & "b" $== "ab"
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.prec.not-looser-than-compare
--- note
NOT binds looser than comparison, unlike C-family `!`. NOT 1 == 2 is NOT (1 == 2).
--- source
NOT 1 == 2
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.prec.not-tighter-than-and
--- source
NOT TRUE AND FALSE
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: op.prec.assign-looser-than-compare
--- source
A = 1 == 1; A
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.prec.comma-looser-than-assign
--- note
A=3,4 is (A=3), 4 — a two element list, with A set to 3 along the way.
--- source
A = 3, 4
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"4"}
===
### name: op.prec.semicolon-looser-than-comma
--- source
(1, 2; 3, 4)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"4"}
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining
--- source
1 < 2 < 3
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:7
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.reports-the-second-operator
--- note
Four operators, one error, and it is at the SECOND -- not the third, and not the
expression. A rewrite that checks for chaining after the whole run of operators
rather than after the first pair would report 1:11 and still look correct.
--- source
1 < 2 < 3 < 4
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:7
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.word-form
--- note
The word spellings are the same precedence level and chain no more than the
symbols do. Before this, the whole of parity requirement 2 rested on `<`.
--- source
1 EQL 2 EQL 3
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:9
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.symbol-then-word
--- note
The mixed pairings are the ones a table-driven parser is most likely to get
wrong: symbol operators lex as `op` tokens and word operators as identifiers, so
the guard has to consult both tables. Consulting only the symbol table lets
`1 < 2 EQL 3` chain silently -- a valid parse of a wrong tree, with no error
anywhere.
--- source
1 < 2 EQL 3
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:7
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.word-then-symbol
--- note
The same pairing the other way round, because a guard can be wrong in one
direction only.
--- source
1 EQL 2 < 3
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:9
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.dollar-form
--- note
The text-comparison spellings are the same level too.
--- source
1 $< 2 $< 3
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:8
===
### name: op.compare.no-chaining.in-form
--- note
IN is a comparison operator, so a second one after it is the same error, even
though its right operand is a list.
--- source
1 IN (1, 2) IN (TRUE)
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:13
===
### name: op.compare.parenthesised-is-not-chaining
--- note
The refusal is about the operators being adjacent at one level, not about
comparisons nesting: parenthesised, the inner one is an ordinary operand. This
is the form the error message tells the reader to write.
--- source
TRUE EQL (1 < 2)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.compare.as-an-assignment-value
--- note
Assignment binds looser than comparison, so the comparison is the value and no
chain guard fires.
--- source
A = 1 < 2; A
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.compare.under-not
--- note
NOT binds looser than comparison, so its operand is the whole comparison rather
than the left side of one -- the tree that parity requirement 1 exists to pin,
seen from the comparison side.
--- source
NOT 1 < 2
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: op.unary-minus
--- source
- 2 - -3
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: op.concat.text
--- source
"A" & "B"
--- expect
text "AB"
===
### name: op.concat.number-is-text
--- source
2 & "A"
--- expect
text "2A"
===
### name: op.concat.bin-wins
--- source
"A" & FROM_HEX("42")
--- expect
bin 4142
===
### name: op.concat.rejects-bool
--- source
"a" & TRUE
--- expect
error E_NOT_TEXT at 1:7
===
### name: op.arith.rejects-text
--- source
3 + "A"
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:5
===

### name: op.text-compare.bytewise
--- source
"A" $< "B"
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: op.text-compare.astral-is-byte-order
--- note
JS compares strings in UTF-16 order natively, which would make this FALSE.
The spec requires UTF-8 byte order, where U+1F44D sorts after U+FFFD.
--- source
"\u{FFFD}" $< "\u{1F44D}"
--- expect
bool TRUE
===

### name: op.logic.and
--- source
TRUE AND FALSE
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
